N["1-1"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point one: undefined and defined terms.",
 "Every word in geometry is built from three words we never define. The first one is a point. A point is just a location. It has no size. Think of the pin on a map. We name it with a capital letter, like point A.",
 "The second one is a line. A line is perfectly straight, and it goes on forever in both directions. That's why we draw two arrows. We name it with two points on it, like line A B.",
 "The third one is a plane. A plane is a flat surface that goes on forever, like a floor that never ends. We call these three undefined, because we can only describe them with examples. Everything else in geometry is defined from them.",
 "Now the defined terms, and their symbols. A line gets a symbol with two arrows on top: line A B. Two arrows, because it goes on forever both ways.",
 "A ray has only one arrow. It starts at an endpoint and goes on forever in one direction. Ray A B starts at A, and A is always written first.",
 "A segment has two endpoints, so it has a bar on top, with no arrows. A segment has a beginning and an end, so we can measure it.",
 "When we write A B with no bar at all, it means the length of the segment. That's a number, like five centimeters.",
 "An angle is made of two rays with the same endpoint, called the vertex. We write angle A B C, and the vertex, B, always goes in the middle.",
 "Here is the mistake almost everyone makes. Look at these two rays. Ray A B starts at A. Ray B A starts at B, and goes the other way.",
 "So ray A B and ray B A are different rays. They start at different points and point in different directions.",
 "But segment A B and segment B A are the same segment. Same two endpoints, same piece of the line.",
 "Three more words. Collinear means the points lie on the same line.",
 "Coplanar means the points lie on the same plane.",
 "Two lines that cross intersect in a point.",
 "And two planes intersect in a line, like two walls meeting at a corner.",
 "Your turn. Name this figure with the right symbol. Pause the video and try it.|",
 "The answer is ray P Q. It's a ray, because it has one arrow, and P comes first because P is the endpoint.",
 "Remember: a line has two arrows. A ray has one arrow, and the endpoint comes first. A segment has a bar. No bar means a length. And in an angle, the vertex goes in the middle. Now go to fang math dot com, Geometry chapter one, and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
N["1-2"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point two: the segment addition postulate. Part plus part equals whole.",
 "Let's start with something simple. Imagine you're standing at the start of a line.",
 "You walk four steps.",
 "Then you walk seven more steps.",
 "How far are you from the start? Eleven steps. Four plus seven equals eleven. You just used today's postulate!",
 "Here are three points on one segment: A, B, and C.",
 "The piece from A to B is A B, and the piece from B to C is B C.",
 "The whole segment, from A to C, is A C.",
 "So here's the postulate: if B is between A and C, then A B plus B C equals A C. The two pieces add up to the whole.",
 "Let's try the simple cases first. Find the whole: A B is four, and B C is seven.",
 "The whole is the two pieces added together: A C equals A B plus B C.",
 "So A C equals four plus seven, which is eleven.",
 "Now the other way. What if we know the whole and one piece? A C is twenty, and A B is eight.",
 "The missing piece is the whole minus the piece we know: B C equals A C minus A B.",
 "So B C equals twenty minus eight, which is twelve. Know the whole and one piece? Subtract.",
 "Now with algebra. A B is x plus three, B C is two x, and A C is twenty one. Find x, and find B C.",
 "Part plus part equals whole. So x plus three, plus two x, equals twenty one.",
 "Combine like terms: three x plus three equals twenty one.",
 "Subtract three from both sides: three x equals eighteen.",
 "Divide by three: x equals six. But don't stop at x! The question asked for B C.",
 "B C is two x, so B C equals two times six, which is twelve.",
 "Let's check. A B is six plus three, which is nine. And nine plus twelve is twenty one. It works!",
 "One warning. This only works when B is between A and C, on the same segment. Here, A B plus B C really does equal A C.",
 "But look at this. If A, B, and C make a triangle, the path through B is a detour. Now A B plus B C is more than A C.",
 "So remember: if A, B, and C form a triangle, the two sides add up to more than A C. The postulate only works on one straight segment.",
 "Your turn. Q is between P and R. P Q is three x minus one, Q R is x plus five, and P R is twenty four. Find x, and find P Q. Pause the video and try it.|",
 "Part plus part equals whole: three x minus one, plus x plus five, equals twenty four. That's four x plus four equals twenty four, so x equals five.",
 "And P Q is three times five minus one, which is fourteen. Check: fourteen plus ten is twenty four. Correct!",
 "Remember: part plus part equals whole. A B plus B C equals A C, when B is between A and C. If a piece is missing, subtract it from the whole. And after you solve for x, answer the question they actually asked. Now go to fang math dot com and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
N["1-3"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point three: the midpoint and distance formulas, and how to never mix them up.",
 "Here's the trick. Midpoint equals mean. Both start with M. To find a midpoint, you take the average, the mean.",
 "And distance equals difference. Both start with D. To find a distance, you start by subtracting.",
 "Let's start with a simple case. Here are two points on a number line: negative three, and three.",
 "Where is the middle? You can see it right away: it's zero.",
 "Now let's test. Add them and divide by two: negative three plus three is zero, and zero divided by two is zero. That's correct!",
 "What if we subtract instead? Three minus negative three is six, and six divided by two is three. That's wrong.",
 "So the midpoint adds. It's the average, the mean, of the two endpoints.",
 "Here is the midpoint formula. The midpoint M is: x one plus x two, over two, and y one plus y two, over two. Average the x's, and average the y's.",
 "Let's try an example. A is the point two, three, and B is the point eight, seven.",
 "Average the x coordinates: two plus eight is ten, and ten over two is five.",
 "Average the y coordinates: three plus seven is ten, and ten over two is five.",
 "So the midpoint is five, five. And on the graph, you can see it sits right in the middle of the segment.",
 "Now distance. Simple case first. Here are the points two, zero, and five, zero.",
 "How far apart are they? Count the units: three.",
 "And three is five minus two. That is a difference! Distance equals difference.",
 "But what if the points are not on the same line? Let's find the distance from A, one, two, to B, four, six.",
 "Go across from A: that's three units. Then go up to B: that's four units. That makes a right triangle.",
 "The distance is the hypotenuse. By the Pythagorean theorem, d equals the square root of three squared plus four squared. That's the square root of twenty five, which is five.",
 "And that's all the distance formula is: d equals the square root of x two minus x one, squared, plus y two minus y one, squared. It's just the Pythagorean theorem on the coordinate plane.",
 "Two common mistakes. First: square each difference before you add. The square root of three squared plus four squared is five. It is not three plus four, which is seven.",
 "Second: when you test whether to add or subtract, don't use zero and two. Adding and subtracting both give one, so the test can't tell them apart. Use two and four instead: the middle is three, and two plus four over two is three.",
 "Your turn. P is negative one, four, and Q is five, negative four. Find the midpoint of segment P Q, and the length P Q. Pause the video and try it.|",
 "The midpoint: negative one plus five, over two, is two. And four plus negative four, over two, is zero. So the midpoint is two, zero.",
 "The distance: five minus negative one is six. Negative four minus four is negative eight. Six squared plus eight squared is one hundred, and the square root of one hundred is ten.",
 "Remember: midpoint equals mean. Add the coordinates and divide by two. Distance equals difference. Subtract, square, add, and take the square root. The distance formula is just the Pythagorean theorem. Now go to fang math dot com and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
N["1-4"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point four: perimeter and area in the coordinate plane.",
 "Here is rectangle A B C D on a grid. A is one, one. B is five, one. C is five, four. And D is one, four.",
 "First, side A B. A and B have the same y coordinate, so the side is flat. Subtract the x coordinates: five minus one is four.",
 "Next, side B C. B and C have the same x coordinate, so the side goes straight up. Subtract the y coordinates: four minus one is three.",
 "So here's the rule. Same y: subtract the x's. Same x: subtract the y's. That's just counting the squares, without counting.",
 "Now the perimeter. Perimeter means all the way around, so add all four sides: four plus three plus four plus three is fourteen.",
 "And the area. For a rectangle, area is base times height: four times three is twelve.",
 "Remember the units. Perimeter is a length, so it's in units. Area counts squares, so it's in square units.",
 "Now a triangle, with corners at one, one; seven, one; and four, five.",
 "The base sits on the line y equals one. It goes from x equals one to x equals seven, so the base is seven minus one, which is six.",
 "The height goes straight up from the base to the top point. From y equals one to y equals five, that's four.",
 "The area of a triangle is one half times base times height. One half times six times four is twelve.",
 "But what about the perimeter of this triangle? The two slanted sides are not flat and not straight up, so we can't just subtract.",
 "Draw a right triangle under the slanted side. Across three, up four. By the Pythagorean theorem, the square root of three squared plus four squared is the square root of twenty five, which is five.",
 "The other slanted side is the same: across three, up four, so it's also five. The perimeter is six plus five plus five, which is sixteen.",
 "Watch out. For a slanted side, you can't just count the squares along it. Use the distance formula, or draw the right triangle and use Pythagoras.",
 "Your turn. Triangle P Q R has corners at zero, zero; six, zero; and zero, eight. Find its perimeter and its area. Pause the video and try it.|",
 "P Q is six, and P R is eight. The slanted side Q R is the square root of six squared plus eight squared, which is the square root of one hundred, or ten. So the perimeter is six plus eight plus ten, which is twenty four. And the area is one half times six times eight, which is also twenty four.",
 "Remember: same y, subtract the x's. Same x, subtract the y's. Perimeter adds the sides. Area is base times height for a rectangle, and one half base times height for a triangle. And for a slanted side, use Pythagoras. Now go to fang math dot com and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
N["1-5"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point five: angles. We'll measure them, classify them, add them, and learn the angle pairs.",
 "There are four kinds of angles. An acute angle is less than ninety degrees. It's small and sharp.",
 "Next, the right angle: exactly ninety degrees, like the corner of a page. We mark it with a little square.",
 "An obtuse angle is bigger than ninety degrees, but less than one hundred eighty.",
 "And a straight angle is exactly one hundred eighty degrees. It looks like a straight line.",
 "When we name an angle with three letters, like angle Eigh B D, the vertex is always the middle letter. Here the vertex is B. Angle Eigh B D and angle D B Eigh are the same angle.",
 "Now the angle addition postulate. Point D is inside angle Eigh B C.",
 "Angle Eigh B D is thirty five degrees, and angle D B C is fifty degrees.",
 "The two small angles fill up the big angle. So angle Eigh B C is thirty five plus fifty, which is eighty five degrees.",
 "Eighty five is less than ninety, so angle Eigh B C is acute.",
 "Now with algebra. Angle Eigh B D is two x plus five, angle D B C is three x, and the whole angle Eigh B C is eighty degrees. Find angle D B C.",
 "Part plus part equals whole: two x plus five, plus three x, equals eighty.",
 "Combine like terms: five x plus five equals eighty. So five x equals seventy five, and x equals fifteen.",
 "Angle D B C is three times fifteen, which is forty five degrees. Check: two times fifteen plus five is thirty five, and thirty five plus forty five is eighty. It works!",
 "Now the angle pairs. Two angles are complementary when they add up to ninety degrees, like thirty five and fifty five.",
 "Two angles are supplementary when they add up to one hundred eighty degrees, like sixty five and one hundred fifteen.",
 "Here's how to remember which is which. C comes before S in the alphabet, just like ninety comes before one hundred eighty. Complementary, ninety. Supplementary, one hundred eighty.",
 "Let's try it. What is the supplement of sixty five degrees?",
 "Supplementary means they add to one hundred eighty. So subtract: one hundred eighty minus sixty five is one hundred fifteen degrees.",
 "And the complement of sixty five degrees? Complementary means ninety, so ninety minus sixty five is twenty five degrees.",
 "When two lines cross, they make four angles. The angles across from each other are called vertical angles, and vertical angles are always equal. Angle one equals angle three.",
 "Two angles side by side on a straight line are called a linear pair. They add up to one hundred eighty degrees. So angle one plus angle two is one hundred eighty.",
 "So if angle one is forty degrees, then angle three is also forty, and angles two and four are each one hundred forty.",
 "Your turn. Angle one and angle two are supplementary. Angle one is four x plus twenty, and angle two is two x plus forty. Find x, and find both angles. Pause the video and try it.|",
 "Supplementary means they add to one hundred eighty. Four x plus twenty, plus two x plus forty, is six x plus sixty, which equals one hundred eighty. So six x is one hundred twenty, and x is twenty. Angle one is one hundred degrees, and angle two is eighty degrees. Together, one hundred eighty. Correct!",
 "Remember: acute is less than ninety, right is ninety, obtuse is in between ninety and one eighty, and straight is one eighty. The vertex is the middle letter. Small angles add up to the big one. Complementary angles add to ninety, supplementary angles add to one eighty, and vertical angles are equal. Now go to fang math dot com and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
N["1-6"] = [
 "Welcome to Prof. Fang Math. This is Geometry, lesson one point six: constructions. Exact drawings with only a compass and a straightedge.",
 "Here are the two tools. The compass draws circles and arcs, and it can copy a length. The straightedge draws straight lines, but there are no numbers on it. No measuring allowed!",
 "There are four basic constructions: copy a segment, copy an angle, bisect a segment, and bisect an angle. Let's do the two bisections.",
 "To bisect a segment means to find its midpoint. Here's segment Eigh B.",
 "Step one. Put the compass point on Eigh, and open it to more than half of Eigh B. Draw an arc above the segment, and an arc below it.",
 "Step two. Keep the same width. Put the compass point on B, and draw arcs above and below again.",
 "Step three. The arcs cross at two points. Call them P and Q.",
 "Step four. Draw a line through P and Q. This line is the perpendicular bisector of Eigh B. It cuts the segment in half at a right angle, and it crosses Eigh B exactly at the midpoint, M.",
 "Watch out. Keep the compass width the same for both arcs. And open it to more than half of the segment. Otherwise, the arcs won't cross.",
 "Now let's bisect an angle. Here is an angle with vertex B.",
 "Step one. Put the compass point on the vertex B, and draw an arc that crosses both sides of the angle. Call the crossing points X and Y.",
 "Step two. Put the compass on X and draw an arc inside the angle. Then, with the same width, do the same from Y. The two arcs meet at a point. Call it Z.",
 "Step three. Draw the ray from B through Z. Ray B Z bisects the angle. It splits it into two equal halves.",
 "One more: copying a segment. Set the compass to the length of Eigh B. Put the point on C and draw an arc across the ray. Where the arc crosses is point D. Now C D equals Eigh B, and we never used a ruler.",
 "Your turn. You want to bisect a segment that is eight centimeters long. Which compass width works: three centimeters, or five centimeters? Pause the video and think about it.|",
 "The answer is five centimeters. The compass must be open more than half of the segment, and half of eight is four. With three centimeters, the arcs from the two ends would never meet.",
 "Remember: in a construction you use only a compass and a straightedge. No measuring. To bisect a segment, use the same width from both ends, and make it more than half. The line through the crossings is the perpendicular bisector. Now go to fang math dot com and practice at your level. See you next time!",
]
